---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { photos } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Current Sky', href: '/current-sky/' },
  { label: 'The Sky in 2026', href: '/current-sky/the-sky-in-2026/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Sky in 2026 — a landmark year of planetary shifts"
  description="2026 moved four slow planets into new signs: Neptune and Saturn into Aries, Uranus into Gemini, Jupiter into Leo, plus a nodal shift. Verified dates and what each change opens."
  path="/current-sky/the-sky-in-2026/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The bigger picture</p>
        <h1>The sky in 2026</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Most years, the slow planets stay put. 2026 moved four of them — and changed the lunar
          nodes on top. If this year has felt like the furniture rearranging, that's because it
          did.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img hero__img--portrait"
        src={photos.skyReal2.src}
        alt={photos.skyReal2.alt}
        width={photos.skyReal2.width}
        height={photos.skyReal2.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why this year is unusual</h2>
      <p>
        The outer planets set the background conditions of an era. Because they move slowly —
        Saturn takes about 29 years to circle the zodiac, Uranus 84, Neptune 165 — their sign
        changes are rare, and when several land in the same twelve months, the whole texture of the
        period shifts. That's 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>The year's major ingresses</h2>
      <table>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Planetary sign changes in 2026</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Date</th>
            <th scope="col">Shift</th>
            <th scope="col">Stays until</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">January 26</th>
            <td>Neptune enters Aries (from Pisces, where it had been since 2011)</td>
            <td>2039</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">February 13</th>
            <td>Saturn enters Aries (from Pisces, since 2023)</td>
            <td>2028</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">February 20</th>
            <td>Saturn conjunct Neptune at 0° Aries</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">April 25</th>
            <td>Uranus enters Gemini (from Taurus, since 2018)</td>
            <td>2033</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">June 19</th>
            <td>Chiron enters Taurus</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">June 30</th>
            <td>Jupiter enters Leo (from Cancer)</td>
            <td>July 2027</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">July 26</th>
            <td>North Node enters Aquarius, South Node enters Leo</td>
            <td>2028</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="card__meta">
        Dates verified against two independent astrological ephemerides, August 2026. Positions are
        astronomy; the reading below is interpretation.
      </p>

      <h2>Saturn and Neptune together in Aries</h2>
      <p>
        The headline. Two planets with opposite jobs — <a href="/explore/planets/saturn/">Saturn</a>
        builds structure and insists on limits;
        <a href="/explore/planets/neptune/">Neptune</a> dissolves boundaries and reaches for the
        ideal — met at the very first degree of the zodiac in February. Conjunctions of these two
        occur roughly every 36 years, and this one landed at a symbolic starting line.
      </p>
      <p>
        The interpretive theme, held loosely: <em>where does the dream have to meet the drawing
        board?</em> Aries is the sign of beginnings and self-assertion, so the question tends to
        arrive personally — what are you actually going to build out of the thing you've been
        imagining? Both planets are currently retrograde and will re-cross this territory, so the
        conversation isn't finished.
      </p>

      <h2>Uranus into Gemini</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/uranus/">Uranus</a> hadn't been in Gemini since the 1940s. Gemini
        governs information, language, movement, and exchange; Uranus disrupts and reinvents
        whatever it touches. The obvious modern read is a period of upheaval in how we communicate
        and what we consider knowledge — but the more useful personal question is which house of
        <em>your</em> chart it now occupies, because that's where you can expect the unexpected for
        the next seven years.
      </p>

      <h2>Jupiter into Leo</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/jupiter/">Jupiter</a> spends about a year per sign, so this is the
        fastest-moving of the year's shifts and the most immediately usable. Jupiter expands what it
        touches, and Leo's territory is heart, creativity, and being genuinely seen. Through July
        2027, the house Leo rules in your chart is the one where doors tend to open more easily —
        provided you show up to them.
      </p>

      <h2>The nodes to Leo–Aquarius</h2>
      <p>
        In late July the <a href="/explore/lunar-nodes/">lunar nodes</a> moved onto the
        Leo–Aquarius axis, which relocates <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipse seasons</a> to those
        two signs into 2028. Symbolically it's an axis about the individual heart and the collective
        good — self-expression and community, the one and the many. Practically, it means the pair
        of houses ruled by Leo and Aquarius in your chart are under review for the next year and a
        half.
      </p>

      <h2>What to do with all this</h2>
      <p>
        Very little of it is actionable in the abstract. A sign change matters to you in proportion
        to what it touches in your chart — which house it lands in, and whether it aspects anything
        of yours. For one person Uranus in Gemini is a career upheaval; for another it's seven years
        of unexpected learning; for a third it barely registers.
      </p>
      <p>
        That specificity is the entire argument for a personal reading over a forecast. If you want
        the general picture, keep an eye on the
        <a href="/current-sky/">Current Sky timeline</a>. If you want yours, that's what a
        <a href="/readings/solar-return/">solar return</a> or
        <a href="/readings/monthly-transits/">monthly transit reading</a> is for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Where do these shifts land in your chart?"
    body="Same sky, twelve different answers. A reading maps this year's changes onto your own houses."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
